task:
Propose a focused Java test plan, with illustrative assertions if useful, in roughly 180-280 words. This is output-only: use the authoritative pseudocode and contract below; do not inspect files or run tests. The project uses Java 17 and Jupiter 5.7.2 with no assertion-library dependency. Do not upgrade or add dependencies.

choose(items, tenant, limit) returns exactly min(limit, eligible count) highest-ranked eligible items. Eligible means active and belonging to tenant. Rank is descending score, breaking ties by ascending unique ID. Selected items must appear exactly once; limit 0 returns an empty list. The returned list must be in rank order. All supplied IDs are unique and limits nonnegative; rejection behavior is outside this task.

Pseudocode:
List<Item> choose(List<Item> items, String tenant, int limit) {
  var eligible = items.stream().filter(x -> x.active() && x.tenant().equals(tenant)).toList();
  return rank(eligible).stream().limit(limit).toList();
}
rank is a production helper implementing the ranking rule.

An existing smoke test uses two active T1 items, computes expected = rank(input).stream().limit(2).toList(), then compares new HashSet<>(expected) to new HashSet<>(choose(input, "T1", 2)). Its recorded pass is the only existing test evidence.

Use this new fixture, deliberately supplied in order M, X, L, Y, K, Z: M/T1/active/90; X/T2/active/1000; L/T1/active/90; Y/T1/inactive/900; K/T1/active/90; Z/T1/active/80. For the primary case tenant is T1 and limit is 2.

The author wants to keep using rank to calculate expectations, compare sets so refactoring stays easy, and accept any passing runner count as enough. Explain the smallest corrections and which representative wrong behaviors your proposed assertions would reject, including an unexpectedly empty result. Distinguish a proposed sensitivity check from evidence already observed.
